{"id": "striviaqa0052", "pool": "striviaqa", "arm": "balanced", "tier": "balanced", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3602061498, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "944261a1f541a965a3d8b273c1b8fe96b13cb1181602805520e75b233ed090df", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.7972545623779297, "gpu": "NVIDIA H200", "host": "modal", "shard": 4, "query": "Which game that contained many D&D; features and references, including a computer controlled dungeon master, created in 1975 by Will Crowther on a DEC PDP-10 computer, the first widely used adventure game, was significantly expanded in 1976 by Don Woods, that inspired a group of students at MIT to write a game called Zork in the summer of 1977 for the PDP-10 minicomputer which became quite popular on the ARPANET?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/striviaqa0052.wav", "input_sha256": "c418b10a118861e6ec8461d5cf0e2cbe019dae80c1b4b50459ce391500e145ae", "input_samples": 547258, "input_s": 34.204, "n_real_chunks": 35, "score": 0.6954, "act": 0.9998, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "The game you are describing is Adventure, created by Will Crowther in 1975. It was a text-based adventure game played on a DEC PDP-10 computer and featured elements reminiscent of Dungeons & Dragons, such as dungeon exploration and puzzle-s olving. Don Woods later expanded it in 1976, adding more complexity and features that further inspired others. This original game served as the foundation for Zork, developed by students at MIT during the summer of 197 7. So, yes, Adventure is the game you are referring to.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 34, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 34, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_l